50 protesters were killed on sunday the  worst day of violence  in a month and a half long crackdown on  the anti-coup movement  demonstrators defied martial law on  monday where they were again  met with live ammunition killing six  more protesters scott hydler  reports  the deadliest area on the most violent  day of the anti-coup protests on sunday  was in leitaya  an industrial suburb of yangon  more than 30 unarmed protesters were  killed with live ammunition  some of the protesters there targeted  factories financed by china  many protesters say the country is  supporting the coup  global times a chinese state-run  newspaper said factories were attacked  and set on fire by so-called instigators  and causing 37 million dollars in damage  it prompted the strongest statement yet  from china calling the situation very  severe and urging myanmar's generals to  stop all acts of violence  punish the perpetrators and protect  chinese people and companies  its large influential neighbor to the  north china is by far  the largest trading partner with myanmar  and is the biggest supply of arms and  military equipment to the tap  myanmar's army after sunday's violent  crackdown in vandalism the junta  announced that at least six areas in  yangon are now  under martial law  in smaller groups than on sunday  protesters came out again on the streets  of mandalay on monday  marching against the junta and  supporting their democratically elected  leaders  ousted civilian leader aung sang-soo-chi  was to have another court hearing on the  charges against her including one added  last week  accusing her of accepting illegal  payments but the hearing was pushed back  today though aung san tsuji's case was  adjourned to  24th of this month  the case it cannot be just could not be  conducted this day today  because we have got no internet  in the country for the whole country  like this six weeks on the protesters  resolve has remained  constant but according to some they've  had to  increase their willingness to sacrifice  freedom from the meaning of the tattoo  is freedom from fear they threatened us  with weapons  but our revolution won't win if we have  fear so we must get rid of this kind of  fear to prevail in our revolution  the term revolution now increasingly  being used by those out on the streets  protesting  but it's now also being used by a  civilian parallel government  saying revolution is needed to overturn  the coup  scott heidler al jazeera
 

